  "sourceType": "CLASSIC",
  "sourceNotes": "Baked custard tarts have been made in England since the mediaeval period, when they were known as 'daryoles'. The Lancashire custard tart is a regional expression of this tradition, sold by traditional northern bakeries from at least the 19th century. The recipe is documented in Lancashire cookery books and regional food references; the defining features are the shortcrust (rather than puff) pastry, the nutmeg finish, and the restrained sweetness. Richard Whittaker's Lancashire recipes and community food history references document the tradition.",
